    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page-wrap">

  <div class="author-box">
    <div class="author-info">
      <div class="author-name">About the Author</div>
      <p class="author-bio">Jordan Hoppel is a strength coach with 16 years of experience coaching 1,300+ athletes. Two-time All-American sprinter. Former Cleveland Indians S&C intern. Elite training background with the Altis track group. 375 lb raw bench at ~190 bodyweight. Creator of the Primal Press Protocol.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>Every guy who walks into my gym and tells me they want a good bench press has a number in their head. Almost every time it is the same number. And almost every time it is the wrong target.</p>

  <p>The number is 225. Two plates. And while 225 is a fine milestone, I think it sets the bar way too low for what most natural lifters are actually capable of. If you train correctly, with the right program and the right technique, most men can go well past 225. The problem is almost nobody trains correctly.</p>

  <p>I have been coaching lifters for 16 years. I bench 375 lbs raw at around 190 bodyweight as a natural lifter. Here is what I have learned about what good actually looks like — and what is holding most guys back from getting there.</p>

  <div class="section-rule"><span>The Real Standards — From a Coach Who Has Seen It All</span></div>

  <p>Forget what the internet says. These are the numbers I use to assess natural lifters based on 16 years of real coaching data.</p>

  <table class="standards-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Level</th>
        <th>Bench Press Max</th>
        <th class="lime">What It Means</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="level beginner">Beginner</span></td>
        <td>185 lbs</td>
        <td>You have built a real base. Technique is starting to click. This is where progress starts to feel real.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="level intermediate">Intermediate</span></td>
        <td>275 lbs</td>
        <td>You are in the top tier of most commercial gyms. You have done something right with your training.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="level advanced">Advanced</span></td>
        <td class="lime-val">315+ lbs</td>
        <td>Three plates. This is a real achievement for a natural lifter and puts you ahead of the vast majority.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-label">Coach's Note</div>
    <p>Most online standards say 225 is "intermediate." I disagree. In my experience coaching 1,300+ athletes, a natural lifter who trains with a proper system can reach 225 as a beginner milestone and push well past it within their first year of structured training.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-rule"><span>The One Thing Holding Most Natural Lifters Back</span></div>

  <p>In 16 years of coaching, I have seen every mistake there is. But the single most common reason a natural lifter gets stuck and never moves past their current number is this: <strong>they are too focused on adding weight and not focused enough on perfecting form.</strong></p>

  <p>I know that sounds simple. It is not. Most lifters think they have good form. They do not. There is a massive difference between form that looks okay and form that is actually moving the weight efficiently. Every technical flaw is a strength leak. And for a natural lifter who cannot drug their way past technique problems, those leaks add up fast.</p>

  <div class="pull-quote">
    "In one session, I took a guy from 135 for 6 reps to 175 for 4 reps. All I told him was to pinch his shoulder blades hard while he pressed. That was it. Ten minutes."
  </div>

  <p>That is not a special case. That happens more than you would think. The strength was always there. He just had no idea he was leaking it on every single rep because his upper back was loose. Fixing that one thing unlocked 40 lbs of strength he already had — immediately.</p>

  <div class="section-rule"><span>What People Get Wrong About How I Built My Bench</span></div>

  <p>When people find out I bench 375 lbs raw at around 190 bodyweight, they assume I built it by doing a lot of heavy bench pressing very often. That is not what happened.</p>

  <p>The truth is that accessories built my bench. Tricep work, upper back work, shoulder work. The right accessories, done consistently, will skyrocket your bench press once your technique is solid. Most guys go to the gym and bench press, do some chest flys, and call it a day. They are leaving most of their potential on the floor.</p>

  <p>The muscles that matter most for a big bench press are not even the ones most people train. Here is what actually moves your number:</p>

  <ul class="win-list">
    <li><strong>Triceps</strong> — the primary driver of lockout strength. Weak triceps means a stalled bench, full stop.</li>
    <li><strong>Upper back</strong> — creates the stable base you press from. A loose upper back is a leaky bench. Scapular retraction is everything.</li>
    <li><strong>Shoulders</strong> — specifically the anterior delt and rotator cuff. These absorb force at the bottom of the press. Weak shoulders mean instability and injury.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="section-rule"><span>How to Spot a Program Built for Enhanced Athletes</span></div>

  <p>Most bench press programs were built for lifters using performance-enhancing drugs. The problem is they do not say that anywhere. So natural lifters follow them, get crushed by the volume, stop recovering, and wonder why their bench is going nowhere.</p>

  <p>Here is how to spot a program that was not built for you:</p>

  <ul class="fail-list">
    <li>Volume is unbearably high throughout the entire program with no variation</li>
    <li>Bench press frequency is more than twice per week</li>
    <li>There are no deload weeks built into the structure</li>
    <li>The volume never goes down — it just keeps climbing every week</li>
  </ul>

  <p>What you want to look for instead: programs with varying volume, higher at the start and lower toward the end of each training block. No more than two bench days per week. Planned deload weeks that are non-negotiable. That is what a program built for natural lifters looks like.</p>

  <div class="section-rule"><span>A Client Story: Dan and the Stability Fix</span></div>

  <div class="story-box">
    <div class="story-label">Case Study</div>
    <p>Dan had been training with me for a few weeks and was stuck at 185. His form was good. His strength was there. But he was not stable under the bar — and instability is a silent bench press killer.</p>
    <p>Instead of adding more weight or more sets, we did something different. We looped bands around the barbell and loaded weight onto the bands instead of just the bar. This causes the bar to shake and sway during the lift. Your body has no choice but to recruit more stabilizing muscles to control it.</p>
    <p>The result was that Dan's internal stability went up fast. His central nervous system had to work harder to control the movement, which built the kind of deep stability that regular bench pressing just does not train. His bench started moving again almost immediately after we added this into his program.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-rule"><span>What the First Two Weeks Should Look Like</span></div>

  <p>If a natural lifter came to me today stuck at 225 and gave me 12 weeks, here is exactly what the first two weeks would look like.</p>

  <p><strong>Monday — technique day with higher volume:</strong> 10 sets of 6 reps at a weight light enough to focus entirely on form. Shoulder blades pinched hard. Bar path dialed in. No ego. This is where the real gains start. Then accessories — triceps, upper back, shoulders.</p>

  <p>The goal of those first two weeks is not to add weight to the bar. It is to make the movement so efficient that when the weight does go up, every pound you add translates directly into strength instead of getting wasted on technique problems.</p>

  <p>Most guys want to skip this part. The guys who do skip it are the ones still stuck at 225 two years later.</p>

  <div class="divider"><span class="divider-icon">Bottom Line</span></div>

  <p>A good bench press for a natural lifter is not 225. That is a starting point. A real intermediate natural lifter should be pressing 275. An advanced natural lifter should be at 315 or beyond.</p>

  <p>Getting there is not complicated. It requires technique that actually moves weight efficiently, accessories that build the muscles your bench depends on, and a program designed for how natural lifters actually recover — not one borrowed from an enhanced athlete's playbook.</p>

  <p>If you have been stuck at the same number for months and you are training natural, the program is the problem. Not your genetics. Not your effort. The program.</p>

